      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand your frustration, and I apologize for the excessive metaphorical detour. You are absolutely right; for USMLE preparation, you need the precise biochemical mechanisms without the goblin distractions.\n\nHere is the step-by-step mechanism of action for ACE inhibitors regarding the Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS):\n\n1.  **Substrate Availability**: The liver synthesizes the plasma protein **angiotensinogen** (a zymogen).\n2.  **Renin Action**: When blood pressure drops or renal perfusion is low, the juxtaglomerular cells release the enzyme **renin**. Renin cleaves angiotensinogen to form the decapeptide **angiotensin I** (Ang I).\n3.  **Enzymatic Blockade**: ACE inhibitors (e.g., lisinopril, enalapril) act as competitive inhibitors of the enzyme **Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE)**. ACE is primarily found on the surface of endothelial cells in the lungs and kidneys.\n4.  **Prevention of Conversion**: By inhibiting ACE, the conversion of Angiotensin I to the potent octapeptide **ang",
